        <div id="sweeteners-explained" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Artificial vs Natural Sweeteners: What Counts
          </h2>

          <p>
            The FDA has approved six high-intensity sweeteners as food additives: saccharin, aspartame, acesulfame potassium, sucralose, neotame and advantame. These are what most people mean by artificial sweeteners. Separately, the FDA has accepted generally recognized as safe notices for highly purified steviol glycosides from the stevia leaf and for extracts of monk fruit, also called luo han guo. Those are plant-derived, though still heavily processed.
          </p>

          <p>
            The two groups are similar in what they do. All of them are intensely sweet, so tiny amounts replace sugar with almost no calories. FDA figures put aspartame and acesulfame potassium at about 200 times sweeter than sugar, sucralose at about 600 times, steviol glycosides at 200 to 400 times and monk fruit extract at 100 to 250 times. The labels differ more than the chemistry might suggest.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
