                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Sugar alcohols such as erythritol and xylitol sit in a third category. They are not artificial sweeteners in the regulatory sense, and many "naturally sweetened" powders use erythritol to bulk out stevia or monk fruit. They are less sweet than sugar and can cause gas or loose stools in larger amounts, which matters if your reason for avoiding artificial sweeteners is digestive rather than philosophical.
          </p>

          <p>
            Then there are caloric sweeteners such as cane sugar, coconut sugar and honey powder. They avoid every non-nutritive sweetener but add sugar and calories, typically 4 to 8 g of sugar per serving in protein powders that use them. Finally, unsweetened powders contain nothing sweet at all beyond the natural lactose in dairy proteins. Knowing these five categories makes a label far quicker to read.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="health" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. What the Research Says About Sweeteners
          </h2>

          <p>
            The safety picture is more nuanced than either side of the argument suggests. Regulators in the US and Europe consider all the sweeteners above safe within acceptable daily intake limits, and a scoop of protein powder contains a small fraction of those limits. Avoiding artificial sweeteners is a reasonable personal choice, not an urgent safety measure, and this guide is not medical advice.
          </p>

          <p>
            Several findings have raised questions. In 2023 the International Agency for Research on Cancer classified aspartame as possibly carcinogenic to humans, its weakest positive category, while the joint WHO and FAO expert committee kept the acceptable daily intake unchanged at 40 mg per kg of body weight; the FDA disagreed with the IARC classification. Also in 2023, the WHO advised against using non-sugar sweeteners for weight control, and that advice explicitly covers stevia as well as sucralose and acesulfame potassium.
          </p>

          <p>
            Research on the gut microbiome adds another wrinkle. A 2022 study in Cell by Suez and colleagues gave healthy adults saccharin, sucralose, aspartame or stevia at doses below the acceptable daily intake; all four changed gut bacteria, and saccharin and sucralose altered blood sugar responses in some people. That is one study of a few weeks, but it is a reason not to assume plant-derived sweeteners are automatically inert.
          </p>

          <p>
            Sugar alcohols have their own question mark. A 2023 study in Nature Medicine from the Cleveland Clinic found that higher blood levels of erythritol were associated with a greater risk of heart attack and stroke, and a similar finding for xylitol followed in 2024. These are associations, not proof of cause, but people with cardiovascular risk factors may reasonably prefer powders without large amounts of either. Talk to your doctor if you have heart disease, diabetes or another condition and are unsure.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE TAKEAWAY</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Choose sweeteners on taste and tolerance first</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">No sweetener in a typical protein powder dose has been shown to cause harm, and the evidence for avoiding synthetic sweeteners is suggestive rather than settled. Stevia and monk fruit are reasonable alternatives, but they are not proven to be healthier. If you want certainty, unsweetened is the only option with nothing to debate.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="read-the-label" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. How to Read the Label
          </h2>

          <p>
            Start with the ingredient list, not the front of the tub. On a supplement, sweeteners usually appear in the "Other ingredients" line beneath the Supplement Facts panel. On products sold as food, they appear in the main ingredient list under the Nutrition Facts panel. Either way, look for the names in the table above, including abbreviations like ace-K and branded descriptions like "Reb M stevia".
          </p>

          <p>
            Do not trust front-of-pack wording alone. The FDA has no formal definition of "naturally sweetened", and "natural flavors" refers to flavorings, not sweeteners, so a product can say "natural flavors" and still contain sucralose. Conversely, "no artificial sweeteners" is a specific claim that brands generally make only when it is true, but check the list anyway because flavors within the same range can differ.
          </p>

          <p>
            Use the built-in clues. Any product with aspartame must carry the statement "Phenylketonurics: contains phenylalanine" for people with PKU, which makes aspartame easy to spot. A USDA Organic seal rules out synthetic sweeteners such as sucralose, since they are not permitted in organic products, though organic powders often use stevia, monk fruit or erythritol. Our <a href="/best-organic-protein-powder/">best organic protein powder</a> guide covers that category.
          </p>

          <p>
            Check every flavor, not just the brand. Many companies sweeten their core flavors with stevia but use sucralose in cereal, candy or dessert flavors, or sell one product line with artificial sweeteners and another without. Product names do not always signal this. The ingredient list for the exact flavor and size you are buying is the only reliable source.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, look at the carbohydrate lines. A powder that tastes sweet but lists 0 g of sugar and a few grams of carbohydrate may contain erythritol or another sugar alcohol, which appears as part of total carbohydrate and sometimes on its own line. If you react to sugar alcohols, that is the giveaway, and our <a href="/best-protein-powder-for-sensitive-stomach/">best protein powder for sensitive stomach</a> guide covers other common digestive triggers.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="taste" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Taste Trade-Offs: Stevia, Monk Fruit, Sugar or Nothing
          </h2>

          <p>
            Taste is where avoiding artificial sweeteners costs you something. Sucralose is popular with formulators because it tastes close to sugar with little aftertaste. Stevia, especially the common rebaudioside A extract, has a bitter or licorice-like finish that some people barely notice and others cannot stand, and buyer reviews of stevia powders often split along those lines. Newer extracts like Reb M taste cleaner but cost more.
          </p>

          <p>
            Monk fruit tends to taste rounder and fruitier than stevia, with less bitterness, which is why brands like Levels and Truvani use it alone. It is expensive, so many products blend monk fruit with stevia or erythritol. Blends often taste better than either sweetener on its own, because each masks the other's off-notes, and Momentous and Just Ingredients both take that approach.
          </p>

          <p>
            Real sugar tastes the most natural but adds calories. Naked's chocolate whey uses coconut sugar for about 5 g of sugar per serving, roughly 20 calories, which is a modest cost for many people but not ideal on a low-carb diet or for people managing blood sugar, who should see our <a href="/best-protein-powder-for-diabetics/">best protein powder for diabetics</a> guide. Shakes sweetened only with sugar also tend to taste less sweet than people expect.
          </p>

          <p>
            Unsweetened powder is the most flexible and the least pleasant on its own. Plain whey tastes faintly milky and plain pea tastes earthy. Blended into a smoothie with banana, berries or dates, or stirred into oats with cinnamon, both disappear. If you mainly use protein in recipes, unflavored is often the best choice regardless of sweeteners, because it does not fight with the other ingredients.
          </p>

          <p>
            Plant proteins make the sweetener harder to hide. Pea and rice proteins carry earthy and slightly bitter notes of their own, and stevia's finish can amplify them. If you have tried a stevia plant protein and disliked it, a monk fruit option like Truvani or a dairy whey may suit you better before you give up on the category.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-should-care" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Who Should Avoid Artificial Sweeteners
          </h2>

          <p>
            For most adults, the choice is personal. If sucralose does not bother you and you like how a powder tastes, there is no strong evidence you need to switch. If you prefer to minimize synthetic additives, the powders in this guide make that easy without sacrificing protein quality or certification.
          </p>

          <p>
            Some groups have more specific reasons. People with phenylketonuria must avoid aspartame. People who notice digestive upset, headaches or cravings after artificially sweetened products may do better on stevia, monk fruit or unsweetened powders, even though the evidence on these symptoms is mixed. Parents choosing a powder for a teenager often prefer fewer additives, and our <a href="/best-protein-powder-for-teens/">best protein powder for teens</a> guide covers that question.
          </p>

          <p>
            People managing diabetes or on medication should talk to their doctor or a registered dietitian, both about sweeteners and about how protein shakes fit their meal plan. Pregnant readers should do the same before starting any supplement. None of these sweeteners is known to be dangerous at protein powder doses, but individual advice beats general rules for anyone with a medical condition.
          </p>

          <p>
            Everyone else can treat this as a taste and preference decision. Try a small tub or sample size before committing to a 5 lb bag, especially with stevia, and remember that the protein inside matters more than the sweetener. A well-made, certified powder with a sweetener you dislike is still a better buy than a poorly made one with a sweetener you like.
          </p>
        </div>

