            9. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Trusting "natural flavors"</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Natural flavors are flavorings, not sweeteners. A tub can say natural flavors on the front and still list sucralose or acesulfame potassium in the ingredients. Read the full ingredient line, including the small print under the Supplement Facts panel, for the specific flavor you are buying.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming every flavor matches</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Brands often sweeten core flavors with stevia and use sucralose in cereal, candy or dessert flavors. Do not assume one clean flavor means the whole range is clean. Check the label for each flavor before you reorder or switch.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Swapping in sugar alcohols without noticing</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Many naturally sweetened powders use erythritol or xylitol, which can cause bloating or loose stools in larger amounts. If your goal is a gentler stomach, check the carbohydrate lines and ingredient list for sugar alcohols, not just the absence of sucralose.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Thinking natural means proven safer</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Stevia and monk fruit are plant-derived, but they are not proven to be healthier than sucralose, and the WHO's 2023 advice on sweeteners covers stevia too. Pick a sweetener on taste and tolerance rather than expecting a health benefit.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a huge tub of stevia untested</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Stevia's aftertaste divides people sharply. Buying a 5 lb bag before you know whether you like it is an expensive gamble. Start with a sample or small tub, or choose monk fruit or unflavored if stevia has bothered you before.
              </p>
            </div>
