            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is stevia an artificial sweetener?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Not in the regulatory sense. Stevia comes from the leaf of the stevia plant, and the FDA accepts highly purified steviol glycosides as generally recognized as safe, separately from the six synthetic sweeteners it approves as food additives. It is still a processed, non-nutritive sweetener, and the WHO's 2023 advice on sweeteners and weight control includes it.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is sucralose in protein powder bad for you?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Regulators consider sucralose safe within its acceptable daily intake, and a scoop contains a small fraction of that. Some studies, including a 2022 trial on the gut microbiome, suggest effects in some people, but the evidence is not settled. If you prefer to avoid it, there are good alternatives; if you have a medical condition, ask your doctor.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is monk fruit better than stevia?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Neither is proven healthier. The difference is mostly taste: monk fruit is generally described as rounder and less bitter, while stevia can leave a licorice-like aftertaste. Monk fruit costs more, so many products blend it with stevia or erythritol. If stevia bothers you, a monk fruit-only powder like Levels or Truvani is worth trying.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>How do I know if my protein powder has artificial sweeteners?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Read the ingredient list, usually under Other ingredients on a supplement label. Look for sucralose, acesulfame potassium, acesulfame K, aspartame, saccharin, neotame or advantame. Aspartame also requires a phenylketonurics warning. A USDA Organic seal rules out synthetic sweeteners, but organic powders may still contain stevia or sugar alcohols.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Does unflavored protein powder have sweeteners?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Usually not. Most unflavored powders, such as Naked Whey, contain only the protein and sometimes a little lecithin to help mixing. Check the label anyway, because a few lightly flavored or natural products still add stevia. Unflavored is the only way to avoid sweeteners completely and control sweetness yourself.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Are protein powders without artificial sweeteners better for weight loss?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Not necessarily. The WHO advises against relying on any non-sugar sweetener, including stevia, for weight control, and sugar-sweetened powders add calories. For weight loss, protein per calorie and how filling the shake is matter more than the sweetener. Choose the powder you will use consistently within your calorie target.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

